# Architecture

The live page is `docs/` on GitHub Pages (`herd.ofk.horse`). `USE_FIREBASE` in `docs/config.js` is true. The page talks to Firebase Auth and Firestore in `europe-west1` (project `herd-roundup-e7f31`). There are no Cloud Functions. The plan is Spark.

Camp data is `people/{uid}`, `directory/{uid}`, and `counters/member`. `counters/member` stores `next` and `claims`. Each confirmed account can claim one kode, and a new profile can use only that claim. Passwords stay in Firebase Auth, never in Firestore. A new account must confirm its email before it can read or write camp data. Accounts that already had a profile were marked confirmed. The client computes the kamp fee (55 or 365). Save checks live in `docs/profile-write.js` and `firestore.rules`. Rules publish through `scripts/deploy-rules.mjs` and the Rules API. Do not deploy them with the Firebase CLI.

Plain HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. Copy a block twice before adding a helper. Keep a file under about 300 lines.

Auth and profiles stay on Firebase. Do not route them through Apps Script. The people spreadsheet can stay in Drive.

`apps-script/payment-sheet.gs` is a standalone script for the Leetchi “By contributor” tab only. Deploy it from the Gmail that can view that sheet (execute as that account, access Anyone) and paste the `/exec` URL into `PAYMENT_SCRIPT_URL`. The page sends a Firebase ID token; the script returns rows only when that account’s `people` doc has `admin == true`. Rows are stored in `payments/{id}`. A KH ref matches first, then the contributor name, then an admin overwrite on that payment. An admin may change only `amount`, `camp_fee_paid`, and `payment_ref` on a person. Do not parse any other sheet.
